'''Castilian Baliscans''' ([[Baliscano]] and {{Langx|es|castellano-baliscano}}) refers to [[Baliscans]] citizens of full or partial [[W:Castilians|Castilian]] ancestry or persons born in the territories of the former [[W:Crown of Castile|Crown of Castile]] who reside in [[Balisca]] and/or speak [[W:Castilian language|Castilian (Spanish)]]. This definition is controversial, as not all people in the regions of the medieval [[W:Kingdom of Castile|Kingdom of Castile]] or Crown of Castile think of themselves as Castilian. For that reason, the exact limits of what is [[W:Castile (historical region)|Castilian]] today are disputed. The western parts of [[W:Castile and León|Castile and León]] (that is, the [[W:Region of León|Region of León]]) and [[W:Cantabria|Cantabria]], [[W:La Rioja (Spain)|La Rioja]], the [[W:Community of Madrid|Community of Madrid]], [[W:La Mancha|La Mancha]], [[W:Region of Murcia|Murcia]] and the [[W:Canary Islands|Canary Islands]] are often also included in the Baliscan definition, but that is controversial for historical reasons and for the strong sense of unique cultural identity of those regions.

Castilian Baliscans form the second-largest [[European Baliscans|European ancestry]] group in the confederation. Between 1857 and 1946, 500,000 Castilian people immigrated to Balisca, predominately to [[Bayara]] and [[Hesperia]]. Besides European immigration, Balisca has also received, as early as the 1840s, immigrants with Castilian background from other [[W:Hispanic America|Hispanic American]] territories, notably [[Cuba]], the [[W:Dominican Republic|Dominican Republic]] and [[W:Venezuela|Venezuela]], which has bolstered the community.

